The provided code is a C# class `LoginInfoCommand` that implements the `IConsoleCommand` and `ITransientDependency` interfaces. It has a method `ExecuteAsync` that checks if the user is logged in and if not, logs an error message. If the user is logged in, it retrieves the login information and logs it.

To write unit tests for this class, we can use the xUnit testing framework. We can create test cases to cover the different scenarios, such as when the user is not logged in, when the user is logged in but the login information is null, and when the user is logged in and the login information is not null.

Here is an example of how we can write the unit tests:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Moq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Volo.Abp.Cli.Args;
using Volo.Abp.Cli.Auth;
using Volo.Abp.DependencyInjection;
using Xunit;

namespace Volo.Abp.Cli.Commands;

public class LoginInfoCommandTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task ExecuteAsync_LogsError_WhenNotLoggedIn()
    {
        // Arrange
        var authServiceMock = new Mock<IAuthService>();
        authServiceMock.Setup(a => a.IsLoggedIn()).Returns(false);
        var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<LoginInfoCommand>>();
        var command = new LoginInfoCommand(authServiceMock.Object)
        {
            Logger = loggerMock.Object
        };

        // Act
        await command.ExecuteAsync(new CommandLineArgs());

        // Assert
        loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError("You are not logged in."), Times.Once);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ExecuteAsync_LogsError_WhenLoginInfoIsNull()
    {
        // Arrange
        var authServiceMock = new Mock<IAuthService>();
        authServiceMock.Setup(a => a.IsLoggedIn()).Returns(true);
        authServiceMock.Setup(a => a.GetLoginInfoAsync()).ReturnsAsync((LoginInfo)null);
        var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<LoginInfoCommand>>();
        var command = new LoginInfoCommand(authServiceMock.Object)
        {
            Logger = loggerMock.Object
        };

        // Act
        await command.ExecuteAsync(new CommandLineArgs());

        // Assert
        loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError("Unable to get login info."), Times.Once);
    }
}
```
Note that we are using Moq to create mock objects for the `IAuthService` and `ILogger<LoginInfoCommand>` interfaces, and then setting up the mock objects to return specific values for the `IsLoggedIn` and `GetLoginInfoAsync` methods. We are then creating an instance of the `LoginInfoCommand` class and setting its `Logger` property to the mock logger object. Finally, we are calling the `ExecuteAsync` method and verifying that the expected error messages are logged.
